 \boxed{\left(\frac{20}{3}, 0\right)}
A given line  
$$
\color{red}{3x + 5y = 20}
$$
Let $y = 0$ since, the X-intercept is the point on the X-Axis   

\begin{align}
&3x + 5(0) = 20 \\
&3x + 0 = 20 \\
&\boxed{x = \frac{20}{3} }~ (Answer)
\end{align}

We have used the above line which is associated with a X-axis value of zero (since, X-axis extends infinitely into the negative side.
Thus, the X-intercept coordinates of the given line is $\left(\frac{20}{3}, 0\right)$
